<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    This file used to name four node IP addresses, permitting cleartext to them
    and blocking it everywhere else. Both halves of that were wrong.

    1. IT BROKE DISCOVERY. Nodes are now found at runtime, from pointer mirrors
       and the public DHT, and a candidate is verified by asking it for its pool
       over plain HTTP on port 80 — the only port that serves it. An allow-list
       of four addresses meant Android silently blocked that request for every
       node NOT on the list, so any newly discovered node failed verification
       and was thrown away. The network could never grow past the addresses
       compiled into the app, which is precisely what the app must no longer
       contain.

    2. IT PUBLISHED THE NETWORK. Those addresses shipped inside every APK,
       readable by anyone who unzipped it.

    The original justification — plain ws:// to LiveKit — is also gone: the call
    feature and all WebRTC/LiveKit code were removed.

    WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT EXPOSE

    Cleartext carries exactly one thing: a node's public pool listing, which is
    public by definition. Everything that matters — the citizen session,
    transfers, messages — runs over WSS, and every message is Ed25519-signed
    with a key that never leaves the device. Someone intercepting a pool fetch
    can suggest a node of their choosing; they cannot read or forge what the
    citizen sends, and a suggested node is still only a DIAL TARGET that must
    pass the same verification as any other.

    See docs/GENESIS_LAUNCH_SEQUENCE.md.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"/>
</network-security-config>
